  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Chronic crisis II -Community Resilience to Climate Change, Mali</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Unconditional Multipurpose Cash Assistance is provided in three rounds during the lean period to the most vulnerable households in the affected communities to cover the pressing basic needs, stabilize the household&#x2019;s consumption, reduce daily life concern and avoid negative livelihoods coping mechanisms. 
- Agriculture-based livelihoods activities are implemented with priority to the poorer villages for early livelihoods recovery and long-term development investments. 
- Green solutions are put in place to increase water infiltration and aquifer recharge in the watershed, improve soil health and fertility required to increased food production and soil water retention that will reduce crop watering with underground water.
- Rehabilitation and construction of boreholes equipped with solar energy pumps and a user-friendly design, to increase the access to safe water for human consumption.
- A business model is set up where the repair artisans will receive a refresher training and equipment, water point committees receive training to oversee the work and vouchers to pay the artisans, and spare part shops are supported with their operational capacity.    
- Community consultations take place throughout implementation, with specific attention for PWDs and marginalized groups. 
- PGI training and sensitization is also included, on topics such as GBV and reproductive health 
- NLRC 510 supports with different data and digitalization initiatives to support branch development. 
- A solid M&amp;E system is set up, including a baseline and endline, mid-term review, and final evaluation.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Almost three-quarter of the Malian population rely on pastoral and agricultural livelihoods and are vulnerable to drought and variable rainfall. Rainfall levels in Kayes have significantly dropped, from 814.4 mm in 2022 to 596.3 mm in 2023. Staple food crop production in Kayes also experienced great declines, e.g. rice, maize and millet, fonio and sorghum. The growing impacts of climate change form a risk for both food security and livelihoods.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tafacirga, Sony and Kemene Tambo communes in Kayes, Mali</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The CCII project aims to strengthen the economic resilience of the most food insecure communities affected by the prolonged drought in the Kayes region, Mali.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The most vulnerable households that are affected most by the drought and low access to clean water. These two factors increase the risk of food insecurity. The communities are selected in three communes in Kayes: Tafacirga, Sony and Kemene Tambo. These are the communes with the most low and erratic rainfall in Kayes in 2023. Special attention is given to marginalized groups, specifically people living with disabilities.</narrative>
  </description>
